"""The hub's front door. Server-rendered HTML, no bundle.

WHICH HALF THIS IS. The hub is two surfaces. Sign-in, the chooser, the domain
gallery, request-access and every refusal are here, rendered on the server. The
operator surface behind the session gate is a Vue app in `hub/web`, served by
`routers/ops.py` and fed by `routers/ops_api.py` (ADR 0223).

AND WHY THE SPLIT FALLS THERE. The instance's consent-screen reasoning applies
to everything in this file (see `venona/api/routers/oauth.py`): auth pages must
not depend on a bundle loading, and pages that interpolate attacker-
influencable text -- an email, a domain name -- keep that concern in one small,
escaped template. Their theme also survives JavaScript being switched off,
because the choice is read from the cookie here rather than by a script. None
of that is true of a surface only a signed-in operator reaches, which is why
that half was free to become an app.

Rendered with `str.format`, so every literal CSS brace is doubled.

The stylesheet is `hub/hub/static/public.css`, GENERATED by `make hub-public`
from `web/src/styles/tokens.css` and the hand-written rules in
`hub/hub/styles/public.css`, which is where to edit. The only colours in THIS
file are its two drawings: the lockup, which the app and the operator app
also inline and which `tests/test_brand_lockup.py` holds to
the kit file for all four, and the Google button's three, which are Google's
own (their brand guidelines) and do not move with the theme.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import html
from typing import Any

from starlette.requests import Request

from hub import billing_state
from hub.assets import PUBLIC_CSS_URL
from hub.field import FIELD, FIELD_CSS, SCRIPT_URL

